,7,6,7,6,7,7,7,7,7],[7,6,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,6,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,6,7,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,6,7,6,7,7,6,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,6,7,6]],"output":[[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,9,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,6,7,7,7,7,7],[7,9,9,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9,7,7,7,7,6,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,6,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,6,7,6,9,7,7,9,7,9,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,1,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,6,7,6,7,7,6,6,7,7,7,7,6,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,6,7,6]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is a scatter of small three-cell shapes on a background. Each shape points in some direction, and the output marks the cell 5 steps away in that direction.\n\n**Shapes and their tips**\n\nEach object (using diagonal connectivity) has 3 cells. One cell acts as the tip:\n- the apex of a `^` shape,\n- or the corner of an `L` shape.\n\nThe other two cells hang off the tip on one side. The shape \"points\" the opposite way. That direction is the negated sign of the summed offsets of the other two cells from the tip.\n\n- **Finding the tip:** it is the cell with the smallest sum of squared distances to the other two cells.\n  - For a `^`, the apex scores 2+2 = 4 and a foot scores 2+4 = 6.\n  - For an `L`, the corner scores 1+1 = 2 and an arm end scores 1+2 = 3.\n\n**Evidence from the pairs**\n\n- **Pair 2:** six `^` shapes. Each output 9 sits exactly 5 cells above its apex, for example apex (10,1) → (5,1) and apex (14,7) → (9,7).\n- **Pair 3:** the `L` shapes have their corner at the top-left and their arms going right and down, so they point up-left. Each corner gets a 9 placed 5 steps diagonally up-left: (6,5)→(1,0) through (14,14)→(9,9).\n  - The odd `L` at (11,13) points to (6,8). That cell is the corner of an existing shape, and that whole shape becomes 9 instead of receiving a dot.\n- **Pair 1:**\n  - The three `^` shapes point up to (9,1), (9,7) and (9,14).\n  - The `L` with corner (4,12) has arms going up and right, so it points down-left. Its target is (4,12)+5·(1,−1) = (9,7).\n  - That is the same cell the middle `^` targets. A cell hit by two shapes is coloured 1 instead of 9.\n\n**Rule**\n1. For every shape, find the target cell = tip + 5·direction (skip it if it falls off the grid).\n2. If the target lies on a shape, recolour that whole shape 9.\n3. Otherwise colour the target 9, or 1 if two or more shapes hit the same cell.\n\n**Test input:** it contains `^`, `v`, `<`, `>` and `L` shapes. The same computation applies to all of them, since the direction is derived from each shape's own geometry rather than from a fixed lookup.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out, \"Start from the input: small 3-cell shapes on the background.\")\n    comps = components(g != bg, diagonal=True)\n    owner = {}\n    for i, c in enumerate(comps):\n        for p in c:\n            owner[p] = i\n    H, W = g.shape\n    hits = {}\n    for c in comps:\n        best = None\n        for a in c:\n            s = sum((a[0]-b[0])**2 + (a[1]-b[1])**2 for b in c)\n            if best is None or s < best[0]:\n                best = (s, a)\n        tip = best[1]\n        sy = sum(b[0]-tip[0] for b in c)\n        sx = sum(b[1]-tip[1] for b in c)\n        dy, dx = -int(np.sign(sy)), -int(np.sign(sx))\n        ty, tx = tip[0] + 5*dy, tip[1] + 5*dx\n        if 0 <= ty < H and 0 <= tx < W:\n            hits[(ty, tx)] = hits.get((ty, tx), 0) + 1\n    for (ty, tx), n in sorted(hits.items()):\n        if (ty, tx) in owner:\n            for p in comps[owner[(ty, tx)]]:\n                out[p] = 9\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"A shape points at ({ty},{tx}), which lies on another shape: recolour that shape 9.\")\n        else:\n            out[ty, tx] = 1 if n > 1 else 9\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Mark ({ty},{tx}), 5 steps from a shape tip in its pointing direction\" + (\" (hit twice -> colour 1).\" if n > 1 else \".\"))\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every shape's target marked.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each object (using diagonal connectivity) has 3 cells."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Finding the tip:** it is the cell with the smallest sum of squared distances to the other two cells."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"If the target lies on a shape, recolour that whole shape 9."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"A shape points at (6,8), which lies on another shape: recolour that shape 9."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"A shape points at (2,2), which lies on another shape: recolour that shape 9."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For every shape, find the target cell = tip + 5·direction (skip it if it falls off the grid)."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"09ff674a83a153d3d3b561e6d1997fbb1939334edead4db51f97e68ce64f1dd4","task_sha256":"98cea55ffd579f55d53e73eca5b9640efff5e28f5599b2478d1dca69f143e639","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
